= i
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**False.** Take the finite, hence countable, <cyclic group> $C_6=\langle a\mid a^6=1\rangle$. Its elements $a^3$ and $a^2$ have orders two and three. A <group embedding> preserves the order of every element, whereas <conjugate group elements> have the same order: $(s^{-1}xs)^r=s^{-1}x^rs$. Therefore their images cannot be conjugate in any containing <group>. This gives the required obstruction. The <torsion-free group> hypothesis in Question 5 avoids this conflict of element orders.

= ii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**False: free rank is not monotone under subgroup embeddings.** In fact every finite-rank <free group> embeds in $F_2$, hence in every $F_m$ with $m>1$. Let $a,b$ freely generate a rank-two subgroup and set $z_i=a^iba^{-i}$ for $0\leq i<n$.

To prove that these form a <free basis of a group> for their subgroup, take a nonempty reduced word and combine each maximal run with the same index into a block $z_{i_1}^{e_1}\cdots z_{i_r}^{e_r}$, with $e_j\ne0$ and $i_{j+1}\ne i_j$. Its expansion is
$$
a^{i_1}b^{e_1}a^{i_2-i_1}b^{e_2}\cdots a^{i_r-i_{r-1}}b^{e_r}a^{-i_r}.
$$
Every interior $a$ power and every $b$ power is nonzero, so the free reduction leaves a nonempty word. This proves the <conjugates of one free generator freely generate> property and injectivity of $F_n\to F_m$. In particular,
$$
\boxed{F_3\hookrightarrow F_2,\qquad n>m\text{ is possible}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**False.** We construct a two-generated countable <group> that cannot embed in any <finitely presented group>. Choose a subset $S\subseteq\mathbb N_{>0}$ which is not a <recursively enumerable set>, for example a positive-integer coding of the complement of the <halting set>. Such sets exist because there are only countably many finite programs but uncountably many subsets of the positive integers. On generators $b_i$ indexed by $i\in\mathbb Z$, form the possibly infinite-graph <right-angled Artin group>
$$
B_S=\langle b_i\ (i\in\mathbb Z)\mid[b_i,b_j]=1\text{ whenever }|i-j|\in S\rangle.
$$
The shift $\sigma(b_i)=b_{i+1}$ preserves the defining relations and has inverse shift $i\mapsto i-1$, so it is an automorphism. Form the <semidirect product>
$$
G_S=B_S\rtimes_\sigma\langle t\rangle,\qquad tb_it^{-1}=b_{i+1}.
$$
Its base embeds by the definition of a <semidirect product>. Since $b_i=t^ib_0t^{-i}$, the group is generated by just $b_0,t$ and is countable.

The <shift-invariant graph groups encode arbitrary sets> construction has the exact test
$$
\boxed{[b_0,t^nb_0t^{-n}]=1\text{ in }G_S\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad n\in S\qquad(n>0).}
$$
The forward direction for $n\in S$ is a defining edge relation. If $n\notin S$, kill all vertex generators except $b_0,b_n$. Every defining commutator then either contains a killed generator or is trivial; no relation is imposed between these two retained generators. Thus there is a retraction of $B_S$ onto the rank-two <free group> on $b_0,b_n$. Their commutator is a nonempty reduced word there, so it is nonidentity in $B_S$ and remains so in $G_S$.

Now suppose $G_S$ embedded in a <finitely presented group> $P$. Choose fixed words $u,v$ in its finite generators representing the images of $b_0,t$. The null words of $P$ are <recursively enumerable>: enumerate all finite products of conjugates of its relators and their inverses, then freely reduce. Substituting $u,v$ into $[b_0,t^nb_0t^{-n}]$ and dovetailing these enumerations for all $n>0$ would enumerate exactly $S$, contradicting its choice. These finitely many image words may be hard-coded; the argument does not require an algorithm finding an embedding. This proves the <enumerable identities of finitely generated subgroups> obstruction and supplies a concrete construction of the required countable counterexample.

= iv
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**True.** Let $G=\langle X\mid R\rangle$ and $H=\langle Y\mid S\rangle$, using disjoint finite generating alphabets. The <direct product of groups> has finite presentation
$$
G\times H=\langle X,Y\mid R,S,[x,y]=1\ (x\in X,\ y\in Y)\rangle.
$$
The commuting relations let every word be reordered as a word in $X$ followed by a word in $Y$; the two projections then show that no additional relations within either factor are imposed. For an input word $w$, form its projections $w_X$ and $w_Y$ by deleting all letters from the other factor. Evaluate each projection with the given <word problem for a group> algorithm for that factor. The product coordinates give
$$
\boxed{w=1\text{ in }G\times H\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad w_X=1\text{ in }G\ \text{and}\ w_Y=1\text{ in }H.}
$$
Both component algorithms halt, so this is a terminating algorithm. No subgroup-membership procedure or comparison between unknown presentations is required.

= v
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

**False.** Use the two-generated <group> $G_S$ constructed in part (iii) for a non-recursively-enumerable $S$. Sending the two free generators to $b_0,t$ gives a surjective <group homomorphism>
$$
\boxed{F_2\twoheadrightarrow G_S.}
$$
The source is a <finitely presented group>, with presentation $\langle a,b\mid\ \rangle$. The target is not finitely presented: if it were, its null words could be enumerated from a finite set of relators, and the tested commutators would enumerate $S$, as proved in part (iii). Thus even a quotient of a rank-two <free group> need not admit a finite presentation. A quotient by a finitely normally generated kernel would be finitely presented, but the assertion places no such restriction on its kernel.

= vi
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

**False, even when the HNN extension also has a finite presentation.** Take the base $P=F_m\times F_m$ and its <Mihailova subgroup> $L$ from Question 4(b). Its <word problem for a group> is soluble: project a word to each free factor and freely reduce both projections, as in part (iv). The base is finitely presented, but the <HNN extension>
$$
G=\langle P,t\mid t^{-1}\ell t=\ell\ (\ell\in L)\rangle
$$
has insoluble word problem by the explicit reduction in Question 4(b). Namely, for an input word $w$ in the presentation with insoluble word problem, set $p=(1,w)$; then
$$
\boxed{t^{-1}ptp^{-1}=1\text{ in }G\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad p\in L
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad w=1\text{ in }H.}
$$
The obstruction is undecidable membership in the associated subgroup, not undecidable equality in the base. Soluble base word problem alone does not let one recognize the pinches needed for a <normal form theorem for an HNN extension> algorithm.
